---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import S from '../components/S.astro';
import T from '../components/T.astro';
import L from '../components/L.astro';
import Cite from '../components/Cite.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Deeper from '../components/Deeper.astro';
import Widget from '../components/Widget.astro';
import SpecTable from '../components/SpecTable.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Software & Inference"
  description="Prefill is compute-bound, decode is memory-bound. Splitting them across a 72-GPU NVLink domain is the payoff — worth about 1.5× on MLPerf Inference v5.1."
  kicker="Chapter 08"
  heading="Software & Inference"
  slug="/inference"
>
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="lede">
      Serving a language model is two different jobs wearing one coat. They have opposite
      bottlenecks, they want different hardware ratios, and for years they were forced to share a
      GPU anyway. A 72-GPU coherent domain is what makes separating them practical.
    </p>

    <h2 id="two-phases">Two phases, opposite bottlenecks</h2>

    <L at={1}>
      <p>
        When you send a prompt to a language model, two things happen in sequence.
      </p>
      <p>
        First it reads your whole prompt at once. That is a large, dense pile of arithmetic and it
        keeps the chip's maths units busy — this phase is limited by how fast the GPU can compute.
        Then it writes the answer one word at a time, and each word requires re-reading the model's
        entire set of weights to produce a tiny amount of arithmetic. That phase is limited by how
        fast the GPU can read memory, not by how fast it can compute.
      </p>
      <p>
        Those are two different machines' worth of requirements. Running both on the same GPU means
        neither gets what it wants.
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={2}>
      <p>
        <T id="prefill">Prefill</T> processes every prompt token in parallel: large GEMMs, high
        arithmetic intensity, tensor-core-bound. It sets time-to-first-token.
        <T id="decode">Decode</T> generates one token at a time, re-reading the full weight set and
        the growing <T id="kv-cache">KV cache</T> per step. Its arithmetic intensity is close to
        one, so it is memory-bandwidth-bound outright, and its only real lever is batching more
        requests together to amortise those weight reads.
      </p>
      <p>
        Co-locating them forces a compromise on every axis: batch composition, memory budget,
        parallelism width, and scheduling. Worse, a prefill burst stalls the decode steps sharing
        the GPU, which shows up directly as inter-token latency jitter — the thing interactive
        serving is judged on.
      </p>
    </L>
  </div>

  <Widget
    title="Where the GPU-seconds go"
    hint="Change the request shape"
    caption="An illustrative model, not a performance predictor: it assumes prefill throughput per GPU is a few thousand tokens per second and decode a few tens, then shows how the work ratio — and therefore the ideal GPU split — moves with request shape. The one measured figure on this page is the ~1.5× from MLPerf Inference v5.1."
  >
    <div data-disagg>
      <svg class="diagram" viewBox="0 0 720 210" role="img" aria-label="Bar showing the split of GPU-seconds between prefill and decode, and the resulting allocation of 72 GPUs.">
        <text x="0" y="16">GPU-seconds per request</text>
        <rect x="0" y="26" width="720" height="30" rx="4" fill="var(--line)"/>
        <rect x="0" y="26" width="200" height="30" rx="4" fill="var(--cyan)" data-bar-prefill/>
        <text x="10" y="46" fill="#fff" data-out="pf-lbl">prefill</text>
        <text x="710" y="46" text-anchor="end" fill="var(--ink)" data-out="dc-lbl">decode</text>

        <text x="0" y="96">Balanced allocation of 72 GPUs</text>
        <g data-alloc></g>

        <text x="0" y="196" fill="var(--ink-3)" data-out="verdict"></text>
      </svg>
      <dl class="readout" style="margin-top:0.75rem">
        <div><dt>Prefill share</dt><dd data-out="pf">33%</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Decode share</dt><dd data-out="dc">67%</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Prefill GPUs</dt><dd data-out="npf">24</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Decode GPUs</dt><dd data-out="ndc">48</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
    <div slot="controls" class="controls-row">
      <div class="ctl">
        <label for="inp">Input tokens<output data-out="inp-lbl">32,768</output></label>
        <input type="range" id="inp" data-inp min="0" max="100" value="78" />
      </div>
      <div class="ctl">
        <label for="outp">Output tokens<output data-out="out-lbl">1,024</output></label>
        <input type="range" id="outp" data-outp min="0" max="100" value="63" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </Widget>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="dynamo">Disaggregated serving</h2>

    <L min={2}>
      <p>
        NVIDIA Dynamo runs the two phases on separate GPU pools and streams the KV cache between
        them.<Cite ids="nv-dynamo" /> Each pool is then free to be configured for its own
        bottleneck: prefill for throughput, decode for wide
        <T id="expert-parallelism">expert parallelism</T> and large batches. On MLPerf Inference
        v5.1, disaggregated serving on GB200 NVL72 reached <S id="disagg-gain" /> over aggregated
        serving for Llama 3.1 405B interactive.<Cite ids={['mlcommons', 'nv-dynamo']} />
      </p>
      <p>
        The reason this needs an NVL72 rather than any cluster is the KV cache transfer plus the
        expert all-to-all. Moving a long-context KV cache from a prefill GPU to a decode GPU is a
        large, latency-sensitive transfer sitting directly in the request path. Over InfiniBand it
        is a cost you schedule around; over <S id="alltoall" /> of in-rack NVLink it is close to
        free.
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={3}>
      <p>
        Wide expert parallelism is the second-order effect and arguably the larger one. Spreading a
        <T id="moe">mixture-of-experts</T> model's experts across many GPUs cuts the weight memory
        each GPU holds, which frees HBM for KV cache and larger batches, which is precisely what a
        memory-bound decode phase needs. The cost is an all-to-all exchange on every layer whose
        size grows with the number of participating GPUs.
      </p>
      <p>
        That exchange is what makes wide EP unaffordable on a scale-out fabric and affordable here:
        it stays inside the NVLink domain, where per-GPU egress is 900 GB/s rather than 50 or 100.
        The 72-GPU domain is not incidental to disaggregated MoE serving; it is the precondition
        for it.
      </p>
    </L>

    <Callout kind="key">
      <p>
        The chain is worth stating end to end: a coherent 72-GPU domain makes wide expert
        parallelism cheap → wide EP frees HBM on the decode pool → free HBM buys batch size →
        batch size is the only lever a memory-bound phase has. That is how an interconnect
        specification turns into tokens per second.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="platform">The rest of the software story</h2>

    <p>
      Three pieces of plumbing matter enough to name.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Topology awareness.</strong> <T id="nccl">NCCL</T> and CUDA know the NVLink
        topology and pick collective algorithms accordingly — including using
        <T id="sharp">SHARP</T> in-network reduction where it applies. Nothing about the fabric is
        exposed as something you tune by hand.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Domain boundaries.</strong> <T id="mnnvl">MNNVL</T> and the IMEX service let GPU
        memory be exported across OS domains; Kubernetes models this with a ComputeDomain resource
        via the GPU Operator and DRA driver.<Cite ids="nv-mnnvl" /> The NVLink domain and the
        scheduling domain are separate concepts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Partitioning.</strong> <T id="mig">MIG</T> still slices a single GPU into isolated
        instances — relevant for inference tenancy, irrelevant to the training story.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="numbers">Measured results</h2>

    <SpecTable
      caption="Published benchmark figures"
      ids={['disagg-gain', 'mlperf-405b', 'mlperf-nvfp4', 'mlperf-ultra', 'inference-30x', 'moe-10x']}
    />

    <Callout kind="caveat">
      <p>
        The <S id="inference-30x" /> figure is widely quoted without its configuration. NVIDIA's
        measured setup is TTL = 50 ms, FTL = 5 s, 32,768 input and 1,024 output tokens on
        GPT-MoE-1.8T, comparing <em>64</em> Hopper GPUs over InfiniBand against <em>32</em>
        Blackwell GPUs in an NVL72.<Cite ids="nv-gb200" /> It is a real result for a real workload
        shape and it is not a like-for-like GPU count. Quote it with the footnote attached.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <Deeper title="Why reliability shows up as a serving concern">
      <p>
        Blackwell's <T id="ras">RAS engine</T> runs built-in self-tests and predicts failures so
        that board replacement becomes a scheduled event. The reason this belongs in a chapter on
        inference rather than one on hardware: the failure domain is the NVLink domain. Losing a
        compute tray removes four GPUs from a 72-GPU fabric, and any job whose collectives span it
        must be reformed. Designing serving topologies that degrade rather than stop — and knowing
        which tray a replica lives on — is an operational discipline this density forces on you.
      </p>
    </Deeper>
  </div>

  <script>
    const dis = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-disagg]');
    if (dis) {
      const fig = dis.closest('figure')!;
      const NS = 'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg';
      const out = (k: string) => fig.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`[data-out="${k}"]`)!;
      const inp = fig.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('[data-inp]')!;
      const outp = fig.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('[data-outp]')!;
      const bar = dis.querySelector<SVGRectElement>('[data-bar-prefill]')!;
      const alloc = dis.querySelector('[data-alloc]')!;

      // Illustrative per-GPU rates. Prefill runs the prompt in parallel and is
      // tensor-core-bound; decode walks one token at a time and is bandwidth-bound.
      const PREFILL_TOK_S = 6000;
      const DECODE_TOK_S = 45;

      const logRange = (v: number, lo: number, hi: number) =>
        Math.round(lo * Math.pow(hi / lo, v / 100));

      const render = () => {
        const I = logRange(Number(inp.value), 256, 131072);
        const O = logRange(Number(outp.value), 32, 8192);
        const wPre = I / PREFILL_TOK_S;
        const wDec = O / DECODE_TOK_S;
        const total = wPre + wDec;
        const share = wPre / total;
        const nPre = Math.max(1, Math.min(71, Math.round(72 * share)));
        const nDec = 72 - nPre;

        out('inp-lbl').textContent = I.toLocaleString();
        out('out-lbl').textContent = O.toLocaleString();
        out('pf').textContent = Math.round(share * 100) + '%';
        out('dc').textContent = Math.round((1 - share) * 100) + '%';
        out('npf').textContent = String(nPre);
        out('ndc').textContent = String(nDec);
        bar.setAttribute('width', String(Math.max(4, share * 720)));
        out('pf-lbl').textContent = share > 0.12 ? 'prefill' : '';
        out('dc-lbl').textContent = 'decode';

        alloc.textContent = '';
        for (let i = 0; i < 72; i++) {
          const r = document.createElementNS(NS, 'rect');
          r.setAttribute('x', String((i % 36) * 20));
          r.setAttribute('y', String(106 + Math.floor(i / 36) * 26));
          r.setAttribute('width', '16'); r.setAttribute('height', '20'); r.setAttribute('rx', '2');
          r.setAttribute('fill', i < nPre ? 'var(--cyan)' : 'var(--gpu)');
          r.setAttribute('opacity', '0.8');
          alloc.appendChild(r);
        }
        out('verdict').textContent =
          share > 0.5
            ? `Prompt-heavy: most GPU time goes to prefill, so the pool skews ${nPre}:${nDec}. Aggregated serving would leave decode capacity idle.`
            : `Generation-heavy: decode dominates at ${nDec} of 72 GPUs, and those GPUs want wide expert parallelism and the largest batch that fits.`;
      };
      inp.addEventListener('input', render);
      outp.addEventListener('input', render);
      render();
    }
  </script>
</Base>
